<%# Two-factor authentication, on the account page beside the login details it
    belongs with rather than on a settings screen nobody would find.

    It is a property of the PERSON, not of the account: the same switch protects
    every account they are a member of, and an owner cannot set it for their
    teammates. The copy says so, because a card sitting under an account heading
    reasonably reads as an account-wide control. -%>
<div class="card" id="two-factor">
  <div class="card-head">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold">Two-factor authentication</h2>
    <span class="label"><%= current_user.two_factor_enabled? ? "On" : "Off" %></span>
  </div>

  <div class="card-body flex flex-wrap items-start justify-between gap-4">
    <div class="max-w-lg">
      <% if current_user.two_factor_enabled? %>
        <p class="text-sm text-ink-2">
          Signing in asks for a <%= TwoFactor::IssueChallenge::CODE_LENGTH %>-digit code emailed to
          <strong class="text-ink"><%= current_user.email %></strong>, so a stolen password is not
          enough on its own.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[12px] text-muted mt-2">
          This covers your login everywhere, including any other account you are a member of.
          It does not change anything for your teammates.
        </p>
      <% else %>
        <p class="text-sm text-ink-2">
          Ask for an emailed code as well as a password when signing in. It is the difference
          between a leaked password being an incident and being an inconvenience.
        </p>
        <%# Said plainly rather than left for the reader to work out. Email as a
            second factor is weaker than an authenticator app, and a product that
            implies otherwise is selling reassurance instead of security. -%>
        <p class="text-[12px] text-muted mt-2">
          Codes go to your email, so this protects you against a leaked password and not against
          somebody who already reads your inbox.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <div class="shrink-0">
      <% if current_user.two_factor_enabled? %>
        <%= button_to "Turn off", two_factor_setting_path, method: :delete,
                      class: "btn btn-sm text-negative",
                      form: { data: { turbo_confirm: "Turn off two-factor authentication? " \
                                                     "Every trusted device is forgotten too." } } %>
      <% else %>
        <%= button_to "Turn on", two_factor_setting_path,
                      class: "btn btn-primary btn-sm",
                      data: { analytics_event: "Two Factor Enabled" } %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <% if current_user.two_factor_enabled? %>
    <div class="card-body border-t border-rule bg-surface-2">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 mb-3">
        <p class="label">Devices that skip the code</p>
        <% if @trusted_devices.any? %>
          <%= button_to "Forget all", all_trusted_devices_path, method: :delete,
                        class: "btn btn-quiet btn-sm text-negative",
                        form: { data: { turbo_confirm: "Ask for a code on every device from now on?" } } %>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <% if @trusted_devices.empty? %>
        <p class="text-[12px] text-muted">
          None. Every sign-in asks for a code. Tick "don't ask on this browser" on the code screen
          to add one.
        </p>
      <% else %>
        <%# No browser name, no operating system, no city. Every other product
            puts them here and they would genuinely help you recognise a device —
            but they are derived from the user-agent and the IP address, and this
            codebase tells customers it keeps neither. See the migration. -%>
        <ul class="divide-y divide-rule border-t border-rule">
          <% @trusted_devices.each do |device| %>
            <li class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 py-3">
              <div>
                <p class="text-sm">
                  Trusted <%= time_ago_in_words(device.created_at) %> ago
                </p>
                <p class="label mt-0.5">
                  <% if device.last_used_at %>
                    Last used <%= time_ago_in_words(device.last_used_at) %> ago<span class="mx-2">·</span>
                  <% end %>
                  Expires <%= device.expires_at.to_date.to_fs(:long) %>
                </p>
              </div>
              <%= button_to "Forget", trusted_device_path(device), method: :delete,
                            class: "btn btn-quiet btn-sm shrink-0" %>
            </li>
          <% end %>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-[12px] text-muted mt-3">
          We deliberately do not record which browser or where from — those come from a user-agent
          and an IP address, and Tastatur keeps neither.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
